<section id="sauna"><h2>Portable 8×13 skid sauna off the deck</h2>
<p>Updated Nov 23, 2026. A wood-fired sauna built on skids, styled like the rear storage shed so it matches the house: light gray lap siding, wide white trim, a white soffit, a single-slope shingled roof, and black windows and doors. You step off the backyard deck by the hot tub straight into a small <b>changing room</b>, which keeps the heat in, then through a glass door into the <b>hot room</b>. It is a <b>portable structure</b>: under 200 sf, on skids with tow and lift points, on a gravel pad with no foundation and no hard utilities.</p>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-deck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-deck.jpg" alt="Shed-style sauna seen from the deck"></a><figcaption>From the deck: changing-room door off the deck (concept)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-backyard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-backyard.jpg" alt="Shed-style skid sauna from the yard"></a><figcaption>From the yard: skids, firing end and chimney (concept)</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="fig2"><figure><a href="img/p-sauna-hotroom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/p-sauna-hotroom.jpg" alt="Hot room with two full-length benches and a guarded wood stove"></a><figcaption>Hot room: two 7 ft benches, guarded wood stove (concept)</figcaption></figure><figure><a href="img/sauna-plan-8x13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><img loading="lazy" src="img/sauna-plan-8x13.jpg" alt="8 by 13 sauna floor plan"></a><figcaption>Floor plan: changing room at the deck end, hot room beyond</figcaption></figure></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card"><h3>Size and layout</h3><ul>
<li><b>Footprint:</b> 8′-0″ × 13′-0″ framed (104 sf), roof about 9′-6″ × 14′-0″ with overhangs; about 10 ft tall at the high side.</li>
<li><b>Changing room:</b> about 7′-1″ × 4′-9″, with a bench and hooks; a 32 in black half-glass door opens onto the deck.</li>
<li><b>Hot room:</b> about 7′-0″ × 7′-1″, for 2–3 people, behind a 24 × 74 in glass sauna door.</li>
<li><b>Benches:</b> two full-length benches, wall to wall, 84 in long: an <b>upper bench 24 in deep at 38 in</b> and a <b>lower bench 22 in deep at 18 in</b>. Two people can lie flat at once, one per bench; three sit comfortably.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Stove and fire protection</h3><ul>
<li>A small wood stove, fired through the wall from the yard end, so no ash or wood comes through the changing room.</li>
<li>Fire-retardant-treated framing and non-combustible cement board around the stove and chimney, with ventilated heat shields, a hearth pad, and the stove maker’s listed clearances.</li>
<li>6 in insulated Class-A chimney with a spark arrestor cap, at least 3 ft above the roof and 2 ft above anything within 10 ft.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Portable by design</h3><ul>
<li>Three pressure-treated skids with tow eyes at both ends and four corner lift points; it can be towed or craned out.</li>
<li>Sits on a level gravel pad with optional removable ground anchors. No foundation, no water, no hard-wired power.</li>
<li>Placed 13 ft out from the deck at the hot-tub corner, with the flue at the yard end, well away from the house and kept off the septic field.</li>
<li>Confirm township zoning, HOA and wood-stove rules.</li>
</ul></div>
</div>
<div class="card"><h3>Plans (PDF)</h3><ul>
